course: dictionary
lesson: 3364
title: Exclamations
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An exclamation is a short, sudden word or phrase said loudly to show a strong feeling, such as surprise, anger, pain or joy. «Wow!» and «Ouch!» are both exclamations. You can often hear the feeling in the way it is said.
explain: An exclamation can be one word or a whole phrase. «What a big dog!» is an exclamation sentence: it starts with «What», ends with «!», and shows strong feeling. In stories, exclamations help the reader feel what a character feels.
explain: The mark «!» is called an exclamation mark (UK) or exclamation point (US). A question mark or exclamation mark already ends a sentence, so no full stop goes after it.
fact: An exclamation is a short, sudden word or phrase said loudly to show a strong feeling.
fact: Examples of exclamations are «Wow!», «Ouch!» and «Help!».
fact: «An exclamation of surprise» is a word or phrase that shows you were surprised.
fact: The exclamation mark «!» can also be called an exclamation point in American English.
fact: English has four kinds of sentence: statement, question, command and exclamation. An exclamation sentence shows strong feeling, like «What a huge cake!»
exercise: Which of these is an exclamation? | Running | Table | Quickly | *Ouch!
exercise: Mum bumps her head and says «Ouch!». What feeling does the exclamation show? | Hunger | Sleepiness | *Pain | Boredom
exercise: Which mark is the exclamation mark? | A dash - | *! | A comma , | A colon :
test: Ahmed shouts «Hooray!» when he wins. What feeling does the exclamation show? | Confusion | *Joy | Anger | Tiredness
test: Which sentence is an exclamation sentence? | Eat the cake. | The cake is huge. | *What a huge cake! | Is that cake huge?
exam: Which mark correctly ends the exclamation sentence «What a surprise» | . | ? | , | *!
